-- Stage 3.B — reconciliation between the shipped schema and the ADRs.
--
-- Hand-written, like 0003: this file exists because Drizzle has no primitive for
-- "drop a CHECK constraint that documents a write-order invariant", and because the
-- reason each statement is here is the deliverable. The DDL itself was generated by
-- `drizzle-kit generate` against `src/schema.ts` and kept verbatim — the prose is
-- mine, the DDL is the generator's, which is what keeps the two from drifting.
--
-- ── 1. DROP receipt_shared_le_read ───────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- ADR 0010 line 68: "No `CHECK` constraint: the invariant is about *how* a row is
-- written, not what a single row contains."
--
-- The invariant is `shared_through <= read_through`, and the mechanism is `GREATEST`
-- in the upsert. The shipped constraint makes the same promise by a second, dumber
-- mechanism, and the cost is not the bytes: a CHECK fires at write time, so it turns
-- "write the row correctly" into a precondition every future writer must satisfy
-- before it can find out whether it was wrong. A writer that gets the order wrong gets
-- an error instead of a correct row, which is the right outcome — but the constraint
-- also fires for reasons that have nothing to do with the invariant. A legitimate
-- backfill, an erasure tombstone, a debugging session that pins both columns, and any
-- future repair path now needs a `SET CONSTRAINTS` dance or a `DROP CONSTRAINT`.
--
-- The rows the constraint would have caught are rows the upsert cannot produce. So the
-- constraint buys nothing and costs a class of future writes that can only be made by
-- disabling a safety net. Dropped. ADR 0058 restates which of the two columns is the
-- mechanism, because that distinction is what the constraint was blurring.
--
-- ── 2. ADD outbox.attempts, outbox.last_error ────────────────────────────────
--
-- Hardening backlog H-18: "delete on successful fan-out; failed rows retained 24 h
-- then purged", justified because "the outbox is the only table whose growth is
-- unbounded and whose rows stop being useful the moment they work."
--
-- With only `published_at`, that rule is inexpressible. A row the relay has tried five
-- times and a row it has never touched are both `published_at IS NULL`, so "failed"
-- and "never attempted" are one value. Retention cannot distinguish a broker outage
-- from a permanently undeliverable payload, a dead-letter queue cannot be built on top
-- of it, and "retained 24 h" has no anchor.
--
-- `attempts` makes "the relay has touched this row" a stored fact and gives the purge
-- something to count on. `last_error` gives an operator the sink's own words without
-- reproducing the failure. Both are NULL-free on the side that matters: `attempts` is
-- NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, so "never attempted" is 0 and needs no NULL branch.
--
-- ── 3. outbox.message_seq: ON DELETE CASCADE -> ON DELETE NO ACTION ───────────
--
-- ADR 0004: "a hard delete must never cascade into `receipt` or `delivery`". The same
-- reasoning reaches the outbox, and stage 3 is the first code that deletes a message.
--
-- Under CASCADE, deleting a `message` row deletes its outbox row with it. The message
-- is gone, so nothing is ever broadcast, and every client that was connected misses a
-- frame with no trace on either side: no row, no error, no gap the client can detect.
-- The outbox is a latency optimisation layered over a durable record; a cascade turns
-- it into the ONLY record and then deletes it.
--
-- NO ACTION refuses the delete instead. That is the whole point — a hard delete of a
-- message with an undelivered outbox row now ERRORS, loudly, and the operator chooses
-- between publishing first and deleting anyway.
--
-- It errors like RESTRICT; they differ only in deferrability, and the non-deferrable
-- form is the one that fails at the statement rather than at COMMIT. Stated here
-- because trap 8 in the decisions index exists precisely because it is misremembered
-- as a delete.
--
-- Both forms are safe against existing data: a cascade has been keeping the outbox
-- free of orphans, so the re-added constraint validates against nothing and cannot
-- fail on a populated database.

ALTER TABLE "receipt" DROP CONSTRAINT "receipt_shared_le_read";--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "outbox" DROP CONSTRAINT "outbox_message_seq_message_seq_fk";--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "outbox" ADD COLUMN "attempts" integer DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "outbox" ADD COLUMN "last_error" text;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "outbox" ADD CONSTRAINT "outbox_message_seq_message_seq_fk" FOREIGN KEY ("message_seq") REFERENCES "public"."message"("seq") ON DELETE no action ON UPDATE no action;